wikipedia_extract: 'Conte di Cavour was the name ship of the three Conte di Cavour-class
  dreadnought battleships built for the Royal Italian Navy (Regia Marina) in the 1910s.
  Completed in 1915 she served during World War I, although she was little used and
  saw no combat. The ship supported operations during the Corfu Incident in 1923 and
  spent much of the rest of the decade in reserve. She was rebuilt between 1933 and
  1937 with more powerful guns, additional armor and considerably more speed than
  before.

  During World War II, both Conte di Cavour and her sister ship, Giulio Cesare, participated
  in the Battle of Calabria in July 1940, where the latter was lightly damaged. Conte
  di Cavour was badly damaged when British torpedo bombers attacked the fleet at Taranto
  in November 1940. She was deliberately run aground, with most of her hull underwater,
  and repairs were not completed before the Italian armistice in September 1943. The
  ship was then captured by the Germans, but they made no effort to finish her repairs.
  She was damaged in an Allied air raid in early 1945 and capsized a week later. Conte
  di Cavour was eventually scrapped in 1946.'
